1
00:00:00,029 --> 00:00:03,980
A unit timer answers a simple
question: how long has this

2
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:05,840
unit been in its current status?

3
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:10,250
Big Board can show a warning, then
an alert, when a unit stays there

4
00:00:10,250 --> 00:00:12,289
past the times your department sets.

5
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:14,430
We will compare two outcomes.

6
00:00:14,819 --> 00:00:17,010
First, a mute ends on its own.

7
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:21,719
Then a warning becomes an alert
while its mute still has time left.

8
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:26,399
In Core, open Mapping and BigBoard
Settings from Department Settings.

9
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:28,769
Find Unit Status Timers.

10
00:00:29,229 --> 00:00:33,940
For the first exercise, Staging warns
after one minute and alerts after two.

11
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:36,119
These are short practice thresholds.

12
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:40,380
Enter zero in both boxes for a
status you do not want to time.

13
00:00:40,790 --> 00:00:44,710
The rows correspond to what a unit
status means, so check that meaning

14
00:00:44,710 --> 00:00:46,469
when you use custom statuses.

15
00:00:46,589 --> 00:00:49,859
This department requires a
reason for warnings and alerts.

16
00:00:50,260 --> 00:00:54,380
That setting controls what you must enter
when acknowledging or muting an item.

17
00:00:54,810 --> 00:00:56,410
It does not change the timer.

18
00:00:56,860 --> 00:01:00,360
Save the department settings, then
reopen the page to check that the

19
00:01:00,360 --> 00:01:02,219
Staging values stayed in place.

20
00:01:02,459 --> 00:01:06,100
On the Units page, use Set
Status on the training unit.

21
00:01:06,570 --> 00:01:08,430
Set it to Staging and save.

22
00:01:08,849 --> 00:01:11,830
The Unit Alerts widget already
has another unit on it.

23
00:01:12,250 --> 00:01:15,650
Look for the training unit by name
so you can follow the right timer.

24
00:01:16,009 --> 00:01:19,299
It does not need a new call to
start measuring time in Staging.

25
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:23,039
Here is the training unit at the
warning stage, shown in amber.

26
00:01:23,420 --> 00:01:25,239
The unit still says Staging.

27
00:01:25,750 --> 00:01:28,999
In the later screen, the same
status has crossed the alert

28
00:01:28,999 --> 00:01:30,759
threshold and the row is red.

29
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:35,919
The colour reflects elapsed time; it does
not mean the unit changed its status.

30
00:01:36,150 --> 00:01:39,270
For the first exercise, the
alert was muted for fifteen

31
00:01:39,270 --> 00:01:40,779
minutes with a training reason.

32
00:01:41,290 --> 00:01:44,269
Open the Muted section to
see that it is still tracked.

33
00:01:44,699 --> 00:01:48,220
The saved alert sheet shows the
acknowledgement and when the mute ends.

34
00:01:48,559 --> 00:01:51,119
You can use that time and
reason in a shift handover.

35
00:01:51,439 --> 00:01:54,449
Now compare the board before
and after that deadline.

36
00:01:54,939 --> 00:01:58,780
The muted row returns to the active
Unit Alerts list automatically.

37
00:01:59,129 --> 00:02:02,149
It keeps the Seen by line and
the reason that was entered.

38
00:02:02,589 --> 00:02:04,309
The unit is still in Staging.

39
00:02:04,740 --> 00:02:07,829
The return is a reminder to
look again, even though someone

40
00:02:07,829 --> 00:02:09,380
has already seen the alert.

41
00:02:09,590 --> 00:02:13,559
For the escalation exercise, the
saved Staging row has a one minute

42
00:02:13,559 --> 00:02:15,449
warning and a four minute alert.

43
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:19,429
That gives us time to inspect the
warning before it becomes an alert.

44
00:02:19,870 --> 00:02:23,049
We put the training unit back
to Available and verified that

45
00:02:23,049 --> 00:02:24,490
the old alert disappeared.

46
00:02:24,910 --> 00:02:28,370
Then we saved a new Staging
status to start a fresh timer.

47
00:02:28,589 --> 00:02:31,379
When the new amber warning
appears, open its row.

48
00:02:31,829 --> 00:02:35,650
Choose fifteen minutes, enter
a reason, and select Mute.

49
00:02:36,159 --> 00:02:38,580
Expand Muted to check the saved result.

50
00:02:38,959 --> 00:02:41,099
The sheet shows a future mute deadline.

51
00:02:41,530 --> 00:02:45,049
At three minutes the warning is
still in the Muted section, so

52
00:02:45,049 --> 00:02:46,549
we know the mute took effect.

53
00:02:46,749 --> 00:02:50,519
At four minutes, the same unit
returns as a red alert even though

54
00:02:50,519 --> 00:02:52,180
the warning mute has time left.

55
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:55,909
Its earlier Seen by line no
longer covers this alert.

56
00:02:56,339 --> 00:02:59,369
Big Board treats the higher
level as a new item to review.

57
00:02:59,789 --> 00:03:03,230
Acknowledge it only after checking
what is happening with that unit.

58
00:03:03,389 --> 00:03:06,869
A community paramedicine team
could use this to notice a vehicle

59
00:03:06,869 --> 00:03:08,300
waiting at a staging point.

60
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:12,149
A search and rescue team could watch
a resource waiting at a trailhead,

61
00:03:12,490 --> 00:03:16,389
while industrial response or security
teams could use their own status

62
00:03:16,389 --> 00:03:18,610
meanings and realistic follow-up times.

63
00:03:19,089 --> 00:03:23,550
Start with a training unit, confirm
each transition, and agree who checks

64
00:03:23,550 --> 00:03:25,189
an alert and records the reason.
